diff --git a/docs/max-tgp.md b/docs/max-tgp.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..8ccead4
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/max-tgp.md
@@ -0,0 +1,40 @@
+# Max TGP: system-owned GPU power
+
+The GPU Power section exposes a global **Max TGP** checkbox. Enabling it makes
+UCC yield NVIDIA cTGP and power-limit ownership. It does not raise the configured
+slider limit, request 225 W, or prove any particular wattage is available.
+
+On a normal boot, the authenticated `SetMaxTGP(bool)` D-Bus method persists the
+`maxTGP` boolean in `/etc/ucc/settings`. Disabling the mode reapplies the active
+profile's cTGP offset, when that profile and hardware provide one. Profile
+changes while enabled retain their settings but cannot write cTGP.
+
+The exact kernel command-line token `codex.max_tgp=225` forces system ownership
+for that boot. The checkbox is checked and locked; the persisted ordinary-boot
+preference is unchanged. Boot without the token to change the preference.
+
+`GetGPUPowerStatusJSON` returns `enabled`, `forced`, `requestedW`, `enforcedW`,
+and `ctgpOffsetW`. The three readings are queried from NVML/sysfs when requested;
+failed or unsupported readings are JSON null and displayed as **unavailable**.
+The GUI polls asynchronously every two seconds and disables the slider if the
+ownership status cannot be read. The Max TGP control remains visible without a
+cTGP sysfs interface.
+
+The shared ownership mutex encloses the actual sysfs writer and both NVML
+power-limit setters (including reset). Enabling waits for an already-running
+write to finish, and all subsequent UCC GPU power writes are rejected. Periodic
+cTGP validation and profile application skip their writes while enabled. The
+boot token is checked by the same guard before any writer can run.
+
+Build and test without installing or restarting the daemon:
+
+```sh
+cmake -S . -B build -G Ninja -DBUILD_TRAY=OFF -DBUILD_TESTS=ON -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/usr
+cmake --build build -j6
+ctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure
+```
+
+Hardware acceptance requires a subsequent authorized installation and boot:
+check the forced checkbox, actual requested/enforced readback, and absence of
+UCC cTGP writes through profile changes and suspend/resume. This change does not
+install a boot service or modify GPU firmware.
